        "city": "Washington DC",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the DC market",
        "market_text": "<p>Washington turns over constantly — administrations, postings and contracts move people in and out on fixed timelines, which makes DC buyers decisive and photo-driven. A rowhouse in Capitol Hill or a condo in Navy Yard gets judged in a commute-length scroll; home staging is what stops the thumb.</p><p>The stock splits between historic rowhouses with formal, narrow rooms and new glass condos with open plans that photograph empty as bare concrete. Both need furniture to make the floor plan legible — one to modernize the traditional, the other to humanize the new.</p><p>DC's relocation clock is the practical argument. A buyer arriving for a fixed-term posting is house-hunting inside a few weeks and often from another city entirely, which means the listing has to be photograph-ready the day it goes live rather than a week later once a stager has been scheduled.</p><p>97% of buyers use the internet in their home search (NAR), and in a competitive market like DC the listing photos decide which homes get a lunch-break tour. Empty rooms photograph smaller than they are, and staged homes tend to draw more clicks, views and showing requests. That's why real estate virtual staging is widely used on vacant homes, investment properties and luxury properties across the District, Maryland and Northern Virginia — it helps buyers visualize a future home without the cost of physical furniture.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Washington home staging, done virtually for each buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage period-aware for Capitol Hill, Georgetown and Logan Circle rowhouses, clean modern for Navy Yard and NoMa condos, and family transitional for Bethesda, Arlington and Alexandria.</p><p>Formal rowhouse rooms get furniture that respects the architecture rather than fighting it — the mouldings and proportions are part of what the buyer is paying for, and staging should make them read better, not bury them.</p><p>Tell us the target demographic and a design style — or let the designer choose furniture styles for the neighborhood. Every image comes back Bright MLS-ready with disclosure guidance, and volume discounts apply automatically: 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+, for investors and builders with several vacant properties.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Capitol Hill, Georgetown, Logan Circle, Navy Yard, NoMa, Shaw, Petworth, Bethesda, Arlington, Alexandria"
      }
